            <ArticleTitle>Comparison of two doses of recombinant hcg for oocyte maturation in obese women (BMI &gt; or = 30) undergoing assisted reproductive techniques.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>603-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To compare the efficacy of two doses (250 microg vs. 500 microg) of r-hCG for oocyte maturation in obese women with a body mass index (BMI) &gt; or = 30 and undergoing assisted reproduction techniques.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIALS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A Prospective, randomized, clinical study of seventy two patients undergoing IVF/ intracytoplasmic sperm injection cycles with BMI &gt; or = 30 kg/m2. Patients with high BMI were randomized to receive either 250 microg or 500 microg rhCG. Blood and follicular fluid (FF) samples were collected on the day of oocyte pick-up (OPU). The outcome measures were serum and FF hCG levels on the day of OPU, number of oocytes retrieved per patient, number of mature oocytes retrieved, clinical pregnancy rates (PR).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Serum hCG levels were significantly lower in patients receiving 250 microg of r-hCG than in patients receiving 500 microg of r-hCG. However FF hCG levels, implantation rates, abortion rates, clinical PRs were not significantly different.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">250 microg of r-hCG is sufficient and safe to trigger ovulation in women with BMI &gt; or = 30.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
